==============================================================================
(2) THE FINE MODULUS: lonely tau* denominator = v_a +- v_b (a BINDING PAIR)
==============================================================================

M(S) is attained at tau* where TWO runners are equidistant from the observer.
At a crossing tau=k/(v_a+-v_b), runners a,b both sit at distance ||k v_a/(v_a+-v_b)||.
The denominator d = v_a+-v_b is the # of FINE sections; the binding pair carves the gap.


[tight AP {1..13}]  M=1/14=0.07143  tau*=5/14
  fine modulus (denominator of tau*) = 14
  closest runners (binders, equidistant from observer) = [3, 11]  at dist 1/14
  speed pairs (a,b,sign) with v_a+-v_b == 14: [(1, 13, '+', 14), (2, 12, '+', 14), (3, 11, '+', 14), (4, 10, '+', 14), (5, 9, '+', 14), (6, 8, '+', 14)]
  gap M=1/14  vs threshold 1/14=1/14:  TIGHT (=1/14)

[covering hardcore 84 {1..11,13,84}]  M=7/89=0.07865  tau*=37/89
  fine modulus (denominator of tau*) = 89
  closest runners (binders, equidistant from observer) = [5, 84]  at dist 7/89
  speed pairs (a,b,sign) with v_a+-v_b == 89: [(5, 84, '+', 89)]
  gap M=7/89  vs threshold 1/14=1/14:  LONELY (>1/14, LRC FAILS here)

[interior-drop6 u98 {1..5,7..13,98}]  M=9/109=0.08257  tau*=19/109
  fine modulus (denominator of tau*) = 109
  closest runners (binders, equidistant from observer) = [11, 98]  at dist 9/109
  speed pairs (a,b,sign) with v_a+-v_b == 109: [(11, 98, '+', 109)]
  gap M=9/109  vs threshold 1/14=1/14:  LONELY (>1/14, LRC FAILS here)

==============================================================================
(3) MIXED-RADIX / CRT SECTION SCHEME: can residues mod different q build a lonely pt?
==============================================================================

The covering-system DODGE: choose, for several coprime moduli q1,q2,..., a target residue
class for tau (i.e. pick which FINE section the observer's gap lands in). CRT glues a residue
mod Q=prod(qi). The question: does there exist tau with ||v tau||>=1/14 for all v, found by
specifying residues mod a few small moduli simultaneously?

We make this CONCRETE two ways:
  (A) Per-modulus "clear-section" sets. For modulus q, the set of grid points a/q that keep
      the OBSERVER clear is U_q={a: no v==0 mod q at a} (the q-witness units). For a SINGLE
      coarse q this only guarantees gap>=1/q at the lattice point a/q. CRT across coprime q's
      does NOT improve this -- a/q is the SAME point regardless of how many residues we name,
      because all coarse grids share the rational a/q. So CRT over COARSE moduli q<=14 gives
      no new lonely point beyond what one q already gives. (We verify the gap is exactly the
      single-q witness, not boosted.)
  (B) The REAL lonely points are at FINE moduli d=v_a+-v_b (>14). Test: pick the binding-pair
      fine modulus d and a residue k with gcd(k,d) handled; does k/d beat 1/14? This is just
      evaluating M at the fine grid -- CRT is the bookkeeping of WHICH fine section.


(3A) CRT over coarse moduli adds nothing -- the witness gap is single-q:
  [tight AP {1..13}] omitted coarse moduli (q with clear observer) = [14]  -> e.g. q=14, a=1: gap at 1/14 = 1/14 (= single-q witness 1/14)
  [covering hardcore 84 {1..11,13,84}] omitted coarse moduli (q with clear observer) = []  -> NONE (covering): no coarse-grid lonely point, CRT has no coarse ingredients.
  [interior-drop6 u98 {1..5,7..13,98}] omitted coarse moduli (q with clear observer) = []  -> NONE (covering): no coarse-grid lonely point, CRT has no coarse ingredients.

(3B) FINE-grid lonely sections (the genuine lonely points), by binding-pair modulus:
  [tight AP {1..13}] M=1/14<=1/14 -> NO lonely point on any grid (LRC holds). fine modulus 14 has no clearing section >1/14.
  [covering hardcore 84 {1..11,13,84}] fine modulus d=89: lonely sections k/d with gap>=1/14:
     k=37: tau=37/89  gap=7/89=0.07865  | CRT residues k mod prime-factors {89: 37}
     k=52: tau=52/89  gap=7/89=0.07865  | CRT residues k mod prime-factors {89: 52}
     => these ARE the constructive lonely points; CRT just names which fine section k lands in.
  [interior-drop6 u98 {1..5,7..13,98}] fine modulus d=109: lonely sections k/d with gap>=1/14:
     k=19: tau=19/109  gap=9/109=0.08257  | CRT residues k mod prime-factors {109: 19}
     k=90: tau=90/109  gap=9/109=0.08257  | CRT residues k mod prime-factors {109: 90}
     => these ARE the constructive lonely points; CRT just names which fine section k lands in.

==============================================================================
(3C) DOES MIXED-RADIX BUILD A LONELY POINT FROM SCRATCH? -- honest test
==============================================================================

Claim to test: 'pick residues mod several small q simultaneously to dodge the covering system'.
Concretely: choose a target tau by fixing tau mod (1/q) sections for q=2,3,5,7 (pairwise coprime,
product 210). For EACH q, a 'safe' section means the q-grid points nearest tau keep all runners off
the observer band of width 1/14. Then ask: does some CRT-glued tau in (0,1/2] beat 1/14?

This is equivalent to scanning the d=210 grid (and its refinements) -- which the M tool already
does exactly via its candidate set. So the honest statement: CRT/mixed-radix is a SEARCH
RE-PARAMETERIZATION, not a new construction. It locates lonely points iff M(S)>1/14, which holds
exactly for the covering hard cores (off-grid) and fails for tight/SDR cases.

  [tight AP {1..13}] best gap on coarse CRT grid Q=210: 1/14=0.07143 at 15/210 ; true M=1/14=0.07143
     -> coarse-CRT grid REACHES the true optimum (true optimum lives at fine modulus 14).
  [covering hardcore 84 {1..11,13,84}] best gap on coarse CRT grid Q=210: 1/14=0.07143 at 87/210 ; true M=7/89=0.07865
     -> coarse-CRT grid MISSES the true optimum (true optimum lives at fine modulus 89).
  [interior-drop6 u98 {1..5,7..13,98}] best gap on coarse CRT grid Q=210: 1/15=0.06667 at 14/210 ; true M=9/109=0.08257
     -> coarse-CRT grid MISSES the true optimum (true optimum lives at fine modulus 109).

